Dear Martin

Unit 4, Weir Lane =E2=80=93 the lease plan

I attach the landlord's lease plan for the estate (drawing WL/04/01, August 2=
026). It will be fixed to the lease, so it needs to be right before you sign.=
 Please look closely at four things, and tell me if anything differs from wha=
t you were shown when you viewed the unit:

1. the boundary of the unit's yard, which is hatched on the plan;
2. the loading bay in front of it;
3. parking spaces 7 and 8, which are shaded and are the only spaces the lease=
 gives you; and
4. the bin store, which the plan puts beside the loading bay.

You should also know where the lease stands. Cardew Hollis, the landlord's so=
licitors, have answered our changes. The landlord has accepted =C2=A345,000 a=
 year with the first three months rent-free, and a break at the end of year f=
ive on six months' notice. In return it wants two things: a rent deposit of =
=C2=A313,500 (a quarter's rent of =C2=A311,250, plus VAT), and a schedule of =
condition of the unit, made at your cost, so that your repairing duty is meas=
ured against how the unit looks now.

Helen will write to you with a full report next week, once the landlord's age=
nts have sent the estate's service charge budget. If you would like to talk b=
efore then, I am on 01632 960216.

Kind regards

